        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Yes, above roughly 4,000 cleaning units. That is PSI multiplied by GPM, and it is the threshold between brightening concrete and cleaning it. A 3,000 PSI, 2.0 GPM machine produces 6,000 and genuinely removes embedded algae and dirt. The 2,500 to 3,000 unit machines most people buy make a slab look better while wet and disappoint once it dries.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Two things close most of the remaining gap to gas, and neither is more pressure. A surface cleaner roughly doubles speed on flat concrete and removes the striping a hand-waved wand always produces, provided your machine has the flow to spin it. And a dedicated cleaner applied through the black soap nozzle and left to dwell does more than another 500 PSI would.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Set expectations on the rest. Oil that has soaked into porous concrete, rust that has penetrated, and simple weathering are not dirt, and no machine on the market removes them. If what you actually want is uniform appearance, washing is the preparation step and a sealer is the finish.
          </p>
        </div>


